      "math_academy_id": "q-149651",
      "sequence_position": 69,
      "problem": "The marginal mass functions of the independent discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ are shown below. Given that the corresponding joint probability mass function is $f(x,y),$ find $f(4,1).$\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th></tr></tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ are independent if and only if \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)\n$$\n\n \nfor all possible values $x$ and $y,$ where ${f}_{X}(x)$ and ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are the marginal probability mass functions for $X$ and $Y,$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, since our discrete random variables are independent, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(4,1) & ={f}_{X}(4)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(1) \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
